      "id": "sort-two-pointers",
      "problemId": "1-two-sum",
      "order": 2,
      "name": "Sort, then two pointers",
      "kind": "better",
      "tier": "premium",
      "time": "O(n log n)",
      "space": "O(n)",
      "complexityWhy": "Sorting takes O(n log n). The pointer walk is O(n): every step moves one pointer inward, so they meet after at most n steps. The sorted copy keeps n (value, index) pairs.",
      "bottleneck": "Sorting costs O(n log n), and you have to carry each number's original index through the sort.",
      "insight": "You never needed the order, only the answer to “have I already seen target − x?”. A hash map answers that in O(1).",
      "code": {
        "python": "def twoSum(nums, target):\n    pairs = sorted((num, i) for i, num in enumerate(nums))\n    left, right = 0, len(pairs) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        total = pairs[left][0] + pairs[right][0]\n        if total == target:\n            return sorted([pairs[left][1], pairs[right][1]])\n        if total < target:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return []"
      },
